# Host policy for the Developer News Dashboard, read by deploy/install.sh.
#
# Copy to /etc/sre-tab/install.env and edit. THE FILE'S ABSENCE IS THE
# DEFAULT: a host without it gets exactly what the repository ships, which is
# what every host got before this file existed.
#
#     sudo install -m 0644 -o root -g root \
#         /etc/sre-tab/install.env.example /etc/sre-tab/install.env
#     sudo "$EDITOR" /etc/sre-tab/install.env
#     sudo deploy/install.sh --start
#
# 0644 root:root, unlike backup-offsite.env: nothing in here is a secret, and
# a port number is not worth a mode that surprises the next person to read it.
#
# This is not application configuration and does not belong in app.env. The
# settings here are consumed by the installer, on the host, at install time;
# app.env is handed to the running containers as an EnvironmentFile= and its
# contents reach the application's process environment. Putting a port the
# application never reads into that file would put it in `podman inspect` and
# in every container's environment for no reason.
#
# It is read as a table of values, NOT sourced — the installer greps it,
# so `SRE_TAB_WEB_PORT=$(something)` is a syntax error waiting for a reader,
# not a command. The last assignment of a name wins, as it would in a systemd
# EnvironmentFile. A leading `#` comments a line out.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The published host port
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Which port on 127.0.0.1 the front door appears on. Default 8080.
#
# ONLY THE HOST SIDE MOVES. Caddy still listens on 8080 inside its container,
# the image's healthcheck still asks 8080, and deploy/Caddyfile still declares
# a `:8080` site block. None of those is affected by this and none of them
# should be changed to match it — the container port is an internal detail of
# an image this deployment pins, and the host port is yours.
#
# Set it when 8080 is already taken, which on a host that does more than one
# thing it very often is. That is not hypothetical: the reference deployment
# hit exactly this and had no supported way to say so, so the tracked unit
# file was edited in the checkout instead — an edit that a `git checkout`,
# `git stash`, or `git reset --hard` silently discards, and that makes the
# next upstream change to that file a merge conflict.
#
# Check first, and check for the whole address rather than the number:
#
#     ss -lntp | grep ':8081 '
#
# The installer validates the value and refuses to install anything at all if
# it is not a whole number from 1 to 65535. A port below 1024 is accepted with
# a warning rather than refused: rootful podman can bind one and this unit
# publishes on loopback only, so there is nothing here that would justify a
# refusal — but below 1024 is where the host's own listeners are, and the TLS
# proxy that forwards to this port is very likely one of them.
#
# The installer stages; only a restart adopts. Changing this and re-running
# `deploy/install.sh` writes the new port into
# /etc/containers/systemd/sre-tab-web.container.d/10-published-port.conf and
# reloads the generator, and the running container goes on publishing the old
# one until sre-tab-web.service is restarted. `deploy/install.sh --start`
# does both.
#
# Tell the host's TLS proxy about the new port as well. It is the one thing
# in this deployment that this repository cannot reach.
#SRE_TAB_WEB_PORT=8080
